  <fileFormat>1</fileFormat>
  <!-- A compilation of stellar models using the Sukhbold et al. (2016) core-collapse supernova yields in place
       of those of Portinari, Chiosi & Bressan (1998).

       What Sukhbold et al. offer is a grid far denser than any alternative (0.1 Msun steps between 13 and
       27 Msun) and a physically motivated prescription for which stars explode, rather than a threshold mass.
       Roughly half of their models collapse entirely to a black hole and eject only their wind, in narrow
       islands of mass interleaved with masses that explode. That structure is the point of the models, and it
       substantially reduces the mass and metals returned relative to a prescription in which every massive star
       explodes: the initial mass function weighted metal yield is 0.0156 against 0.0290 for Portinari, Chiosi &
       Bressan (1998).

       THE YIELDS DO NOT DEPEND ON METALLICITY. Sukhbold et al. computed their models at a single metallicity,
       Solar, and for non-rotating progenitors only. The included file writes each model twice, at two
       bracketing metallicities with identical yields, which states that fact explicitly and keeps the
       interpolation well posed. Any element supplied only by this file therefore takes its Solar yield at every
       metallicity. This compilation is appropriate for exploring the consequences of the explodability
       prescription, which is its purpose, but it is not a metallicity-dependent yield set and should not be
       used to study metallicity trends in abundances; use the Limongi & Chieffi (2018) compilation for that.

       The included file uses the W18 neutrino engine; swap it for stellarPropertiesSukhbold2016_N20.xml to use
       the N20 engine instead, which explodes more of the grid.

       Sukhbold et al. tabulate no lifetimes, so the Portinari, Chiosi & Bressan (1998) lifetimes are included;
       their yields deliberately are not, since those cover 9-120 Msun and would overlap the Sukhbold models.
       Marigo (2001) supplies the asymptotic giant branch yields below 9 Msun. -->
  <xi:include href="stellarPropertiesMarigo2001.xml"                          xpointer="xpointer(stars/star)" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude"/>
  <xi:include href="stellarPropertiesHegerWoosley2002.xml"                    xpointer="xpointer(stars/star)" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude"/>
  <xi:include href="stellarPropertiesTumlinsonShullVenkatesan2003.xml"        xpointer="xpointer(stars/star)" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude"/>
  <xi:include href="stellarPropertiesPortinariChiosiBressan1998Lifetimes.xml" xpointer="xpointer(stars/star)" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude"/>
  <xi:include href="stellarPropertiesSukhbold2016_W18.xml"                    xpointer="xpointer(stars/star)" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude"/>
</stars>
